        <div id="real-vs-fake" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Real Car ACs vs the Fakes Flooding Amazon
          </h2>

          <p>
            Search for a portable air conditioner for a vehicle and the first page of results is mostly not air conditioners. You will see small boxes with a water tank, a fan and a foam pad, sold as a car window air conditioner or a 12V car AC for a fraction of the price of anything in our list. Many are labeled "mini air conditioner," "personal AC" or "air cooler," and the photos show frost and blue light. None of them contain a compressor or refrigerant.
          </p>

          <p>
            What they are is evaporative coolers, sometimes called swamp coolers, or simply fans blowing over ice. An evaporative cooler works by evaporating water, which can drop the air temperature a few degrees in dry desert air. It also adds moisture. In a closed car in a humid climate, that moisture makes the cabin feel muggier, not cooler, and fogs the windows. If you want one for a dry climate and an open space, our <a href="/best-evaporative-cooler/">evaporative cooler guide</a> explains where they work, but a car is rarely that place.
          </p>

          <p>
            The physics settles the argument. A real air conditioner moves heat from inside to outside, and removing 5,000 BTU per hour of heat, about 1,465 watts of thermal energy, needs roughly 300 to 500 watts of electricity with today's compressors. A device plugged into a car's cigarette lighter socket can draw at most about 120 to 180 watts, and most of the fake units draw 10 to 60. There is no way to move meaningful heat out of a car with that much power. If a listing has no BTU rating, no exhaust hose and a water tank, it is not an air conditioner.
          </p>

          <p>
            The units we rank all share three traits: a sealed refrigerant circuit with a compressor, at least one hose or a roof unit that sends the heat outside, and a stated cooling capacity in BTU or watts. That makes them expensive, heavy and power-hungry, which is exactly why the fakes exist. There is no cheap shortcut. A battery powered portable air conditioner that actually cools costs from several hundred dollars for the unit alone, and more once you add a battery.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE QUICK TEST</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">No compressor, no exhaust hose, no BTU number: it is a fan.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Real portable car ACs list cooling in BTU or watts, vent hot air outside, and draw hundreds of watts. Anything with a water tank that plugs into a USB port or 12V socket and claims to be an air conditioner is an evaporative cooler at best.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="how-much-cooling" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Much Cooling a Car Actually Needs
          </h2>

          <p>
            A car is one of the hardest spaces to cool. It is mostly glass, it has almost no insulation, and a dark car parked in direct sun can reach cabin temperatures well above 130 degrees. That is why the engine-driven AC in a typical passenger car is far more powerful than any portable unit here, and why it still takes several minutes to cool a hot car on the road. No battery unit is going to match it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Portable compressor units sit between 2,300 and about 6,100 BTU. In practice, that range handles a parked car, SUV or van in the shade, in the evening or overnight, when the heat load is mostly the air inside and the occupants. It struggles in midday sun with the windows bare. The fix is not a bigger unit; it is cutting the heat load with reflective window covers, a sunshade on the windshield and parking in shade, which can matter as much as an extra 1,000 BTU.
          </p>

          <p>
            As a rough guide, a sedan or small hatchback used as a sleeping space is well served by 2,300 to 5,000 BTU at night. A midsize SUV or minivan with the seats folded wants 5,000 BTU or more. A full-size van with a high roof and lots of glass is at the edge of what a portable can do, and is where a permanently installed rooftop unit such as the Velit starts to make sense. A truck sleeper cab is a smaller space but has a roof that bakes, which is why the RTX 2000 is rated around 6,800 BTU.
          </p>

          <p>
            Body heat counts too. Each person adds roughly 300 to 400 BTU per hour at rest, and a dog adds more than you might expect. Two adults and a large dog in a minivan add close to 1,000 BTU of load before the sun is involved. That is one reason the smallest units feel underpowered with a full car and fine with one sleeper.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers That Matter</p>
