        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do 12V car air conditioners really work?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Real ones do, but they are not the cheap gadgets most listings show. A true 12V or battery AC has a compressor, refrigerant, an exhaust hose and a BTU rating, and draws 200 to 700 watts. Units with a water tank that plug into a car socket and draw under 60 watts are evaporative coolers or fans and cannot meaningfully cool a car.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run a portable air conditioner from my car's cigarette lighter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not at full power. Most car accessory sockets are fused at 10 to 15 amps, about 120 to 180 watts, while a compressor AC needs 300 watts or more to cool. The socket is useful for slowly charging the AC's battery while you drive. To run the unit, use its battery, a power station or a hard-wired house bank.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a battery powered portable air conditioner run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Divide usable watt-hours by average draw. A 1,024Wh battery gives about 870Wh usable; at a 300 watt average that is just under three hours, at 150 watts on eco about six. Pre-cooling on shore power, covering the windows and choosing a modest target temperature are the easiest ways to stretch runtime through a night.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best portable AC for sleeping in a car?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most SUVs and minivans, the EcoFlow WAVE 3 or Zero Breeze Mark 3 with a 1kWh battery covers a few hours of real cooling and more with a second battery or power station. In a small sedan, the Zero Breeze Mark 2 fits better. Window covers matter as much as the unit for comfortable sleep.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a car window air conditioner better than a portable one?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most products sold as car window air conditioners are evaporative coolers that hang on the glass, not compressor ACs. A real portable compressor unit with a sealed window insert for its hoses will cool far better. True window-mounted compressor units are rare for cars because car windows are curved and short.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a portable car AC keep my dog cool in a parked car?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can help for short periods while you stay nearby, in shade, with the windows covered. It is not a safety system: batteries run out and compressors can shut off when they overheat. Use a temperature monitor with phone alerts, and never leave a pet for long in a hot car regardless of the AC.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I idle my car to run a portable AC instead?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. If the engine is running, the car's built-in AC is far more powerful and efficient than a portable plugged into an inverter. Idling also burns fuel, is restricted in many places and is dangerous in any enclosed space because of carbon monoxide. Portable units make sense when the engine is off.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

